{"description":"Previously, we reused the radicle key to create the iroh endpoint, which is also based on an ed25519 keypair. The appeal was minimalism: one key, one identity, and since the endpoint ID is derivable from the public key, `iroh://` URIs could stay bare and be resolved straight from the author DID.\n\nThat coupling has some costs worth being explicit about:\n\n- **ssh-agent contract.** radicle keeps the private key out of process memory by delegating signing to ssh-agent. Reusing the key for iroh breaks that — establishing the endpoint requires the raw private key, so we ended up prompting for the passphrase and loading it into memory.\n- **One DID, one endpoint.** Tying the endpoint ID to the DID means a user can only ever have a single serving endpoint. In practice you might want to serve the same identity from both radicle-desktop and an always-on CLI host.\n- **Future-proofing.** radicle itself plans to decouple the user DID from the node ID in the mid-term; reusing the radicle key now would mean reworking this again later.\n- **Blast radius.** Separate keys for separate purposes is generally good hygiene — a leak of the iroh key shouldn't compromise the radicle identity, and vice versa.\n\nThe trade-offs going the other way are real but smaller:\n\n- There's now a second key to manage and back up alongside the radicle key.\n- Endpoint IDs can no longer be derived from the DID, so the serve command writes the full endpoint ID into the \\`iroh://\\` location URI. For backwards compatibility with artifacts produced by older versions of the CLI, bare \\`iroh://\\` URIs (without an embedded endpoint ID) continue to be resolved via the old DID-derivation logic.\n\nGiven the balance, this patch introduces a new ed25519 key stored in the radicle home directory and uses it to create the iroh endpoint.","revision":"c884af86bfb218bd0cc98a2beadc8948f487a9f1","type":"revision.edit"}